From: NeoDarwin
Subject: [PATCH] bsd_init: no sealed-root authentication on SBSA

An Apple silicon macOS kernel rooted on anything but a ramdisk requires
the root to be a sealed system volume: bsd_init() asks the root file
system to authenticate itself (FSIOC_KERNEL_ROOTAUTH, which APFS answers
for a signed snapshot) and panics otherwise ("rootvp not authenticated
after mounting"). NeoDarwin's disk root (P1-10, docs/kernel/storage.md) is
an ordinary journaled HFS+ volume, which has no seal and doesn't implement
the ioctl. For GENERIC_ARM64_PLATFORM the check is skipped; a sealed root
of NeoDarwin's own is later work (the signed system image, Phase 3), and
other board configs keep Apple's rule. The ramdisk roots never reached the
check (bsd_rooted_ramdisk()). Found at the first boot from virtio-blk.

Rebase-risk: low (one condition and an include).
---
--- a/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c
@@ -383,6 +383,10 @@
 	strlcpy(p->p_comm, s, sizeof(p->p_comm));
 	strlcpy(p->p_name, s, sizeof(p->p_name));
 }
+
+#if defined(__arm64__)
+#include <pexpert/arm64/board_config.h>
+#endif
 
 /* To allow these values to be patched, they're globals here */
 #include <machine/vmparam.h>
@@ -938,7 +942,13 @@
 	if (!bsd_rooted_ramdisk()) {
 		boolean_t require_rootauth = FALSE;
 
-#if XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX && defined(__arm64__)
+#if XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX && defined(__arm64__) && GENERIC_ARM64_PLATFORM
+		/*
+		 * NeoDarwin: the root is an ordinary writable HFS+ volume, not a
+		 * sealed system volume snapshot; nothing can authenticate it yet.
+		 */
+		require_rootauth = FALSE;
+#elif XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX && defined(__arm64__)
 #if CONFIG_IMAGEBOOT
 		/* Apple Silicon MacOS */
 		require_rootauth = !imageboot_desired();
